00:15:50,780 --> 00:15:54,780
So, she's an art conservator.

331
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:58,280
All these people are people

332
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:01,780
who have reputations as high-minded

333
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:06,780
intellectuals and members of our community.

334
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:09,280
I don't know that Yale is

335
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:11,780
in strong need of a board of

336
00:16:11,780 --> 00:16:12,780
directors.

337
00:16:12,780 --> 00:16:14,780
I think we would function like an orchestra without a

338
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:15,780
conductor.

339
00:16:15,780 --> 00:16:16,780
We could do all right.

340
00:16:16,780 --> 00:16:20,780
But it imposes on any corporation a sort of

341
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:27,780
regulatory standard that is important.

342
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:37,780
So, a board of directors has a really important function,

343
00:16:37,780 --> 00:16:39,780
because let's move away from the nonprofit.

344
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:42,780
Yale is a nonprofit organization.

345
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:45,280
Let's consider someone doing

346
00:16:45,280 --> 00:16:47,780
business, a for-profit corporation.

347
00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:54,780
There are way too many opportunities for sleazy behavior.

348
00:16:54,780 --> 00:17:01,280
And you only are willing to invest in a

349
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:07,780
company if the sleazy behavior is somehow under control.

350
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:12,780
So, I'm going to talk about maybe the

351
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:17,780
most important kind of sleazy behavior in a company.

352
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:20,780
It's called tunneling.

353
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:25,280
Tunneling is sneaking away with value, putting it in

354
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:29,780
your own pocket if you work for a company,

355
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:31,780
rather than in the stockholders'.

356
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:33,780
The stockholders own the company.

357
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:35,780
They're supposed to get the money.

358
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:37,780
But the employees have never met the stockholders.

359
00:17:37,780 --> 00:17:41,280
And they sometimes think, you know, there's

360
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:44,780
a lot of things we can do.

361
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:48,780
And let's do them.

362
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:51,780
And the money is going to come from

363
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:54,780
the stockholders and somehow end up in our pockets.

364
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:58,780
So, I'll give you just examples of tunneling.

365
00:17:58,780 --> 00:18:01,280
The company has some asset and

366
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,780
it's going to sell it, right?

367
00:18:03,780 --> 00:18:05,780
So, asset sales.

368
00:18:05,780 --> 00:18:07,780
And part of the doing business.

369
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:09,780
How does the company make sure

370
00:18:09,780 --> 00:18:11,780
that it gets a good price?

371
00:18:11,780 --> 00:18:14,780
Well, if you work for a company and you're

372
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:17,780
in charge of the asset sale, you can tunnel.

373
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:20,280
You say, I'll sell it to

374
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:22,780
my brother-in-law at a really low price.

375
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:23,780
All right?

376
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:25,780
He'll wait a while and

377
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:27,780
then he'll pay me off later.

378
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:28,780
Okay?

379
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:30,780
That's hard to detect.

380
00:18:30,780 --> 00:18:32,780
Because how do you know if you are a stockholder?

381
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:34,780
How are you going to find out?

382
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:36,780
That's stealing from the company, right?

383
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:40,780
If you sell it at a non-market price.

384
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:42,780
How does somebody know that?

385
00:18:42,780 --> 00:18:47,780
I mean, the stockholders can't be looking at every deal.

386
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:49,780
So, I'll give you another example.

387
00:18:49,780 --> 00:18:51,780
Contracts.

388
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:54,780
The company is signing a contract

389
00:18:54,780 --> 00:18:57,780
to provide something to them regularly.

390
00:18:57,780 --> 00:19:00,780
They can pay too much.

391
00:19:00,780 --> 00:19:02,780
Now, it could be your brother-in-law who's running.

392
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:05,780
But, hey, let's not be so obvious about it.

393
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,780
It'll just be some guy that I met.

394
00:19:07,780 --> 00:19:10,780
And we're both anti-social personalities.

395
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:12,780
We've just discovered each other.

396
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:14,280
And, you know, it doesn't

397
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:15,780
even have to be written down.

398
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:18,780
I'll give this contract to you over somebody else.

399
00:19:18,780 --> 00:19:20,780
And I'll pay you too much.

400
00:19:20,780 --> 00:19:24,780
And, you know, sometime later, you take care of me.

401
00:19:24,780 --> 00:19:26,780
You know, I did it for you.

402
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:28,780
You return the favor.

403
00:19:28,780 --> 00:19:30,780
This is tunneling.

404
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:31,780
Another one.

405
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:33,780
Executive compensation.

406
00:19:35,780 --> 00:19:40,780
The CEO decides to pay his friends

407
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:45,780
in the company, his cronies, very high salaries.

408
00:19:45,780 --> 00:19:48,780
And he would defend it if anyone

409
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:51,780
asked, but these people are worth every penny.

410
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:53,780
Who knows what somebody is worth?

411
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:55,780
Who's going to judge that?

412
00:19:55,780 --> 00:20:03,780
Expropriation of corporate opportunities.

413
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:04,780
Expropriation of corporate opportunities.

414
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:05,780
Expropriation of corporate opportunities.

415
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:07,780
Expropriation of corporate opportunities.

416
00:20:07,780 --> 00:20:10,780
See, one of the most valuable things about

417
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:13,780
a company is that you're in the business,

418
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:15,780
and you know what's happening, right?

419
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:18,780
And your company is producing a certain kind of product.

420
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:20,780
Then someone comes along and says, you

421
00:20:20,780 --> 00:20:22,780
know, I don't really want your product,

422
00:20:22,780 --> 00:20:24,780
but you seem like the kind of people

423
00:20:24,780 --> 00:20:26,780
who could produce something that I really need.

424
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:28,780
Something else.

425
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:31,780
And so, you as a director, as

426
00:20:31,780 --> 00:20:34,780
a president of a company, you should,

427
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:37,780
you should say, okay, we can do it.

428
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:39,780
But you don't do that.

429
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:41,780
Instead you say, I'm setting up

430
00:20:41,780 --> 00:20:43,780
a new company with my name.

431
00:20:43,780 --> 00:20:46,780
Or my brother-in-law is setting up a new company.

432
00:20:46,780 --> 00:20:48,780
And you tell him all the corporate

433
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:50,780
secrets and how he can do it.

434
00:20:50,780 --> 00:20:51,780
Okay?

435
00:20:51,780 --> 00:20:55,780
That's tunneling, again.

436
00:20:55,780 --> 00:20:58,780
And of course, there's insider trading.

437
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:02,780
That's when people in the company,

438
00:21:02,780 --> 00:21:06,280
will buy or sell their own

439
00:21:06,280 --> 00:21:09,780
shares based on knowledge that they have,

440
00:21:09,780 --> 00:21:12,780
that they haven't told the public yet.

441
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:15,280
So, if you know that the company is

442
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:17,780
going to collapse, you sell your shares right away.

443
00:21:17,780 --> 00:21:19,780
If you know the company has some great

444
00:21:19,780 --> 00:21:21,780
news, you buy them before you announce it.

445
00:21:21,780 --> 00:21:23,780
That's tunneling, again, because you're

446
00:21:23,780 --> 00:21:25,780
taking money out of the company

447
00:21:25,780 --> 00:21:27,780
because of the opportunities you have as

448
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:29,780
part of the management of the company.

449
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:31,780
And you're not going to sell your shares.

450
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:33,280
You're going to take money out

451
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:34,780
of the management of the company.

452
00:21:34,780 --> 00:21:36,280
You see how many different ways,

453
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:37,780
and I haven't listed them all.

454
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:39,780
There's many, many ways.

455
00:21:39,780 --> 00:21:41,780
Companies do so many things.

456
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:43,780
So, why would you ever invest in a company?

457
00:21:43,780 --> 00:21:45,780
You might think, there's a million ways

458
00:21:45,780 --> 00:21:47,780
to get money out of a company.

459
00:21:47,780 --> 00:21:50,780
And the people who are running it don't know me.

460
00:21:50,780 --> 00:21:52,780
They don't care about me.

461
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:54,780
That's why a corporation is kind of

462
00:21:54,780 --> 00:21:56,780
a delicate thing to work at all.

463
00:21:56,780 --> 00:21:58,280
It has to have some

464
00:21:58,280 --> 00:21:59,780
way of controlling all these things.

465
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:00,780
And there's so many of them.

466
00:22:00,780 --> 00:22:01,780
And they're so complex.

467
00:22:01,780 --> 00:22:02,780
And they're so complex.

468
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:04,780
I would say, that's why we have boards of directors,

469
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:09,780
and why we put people of

470
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:14,780
known reputations on boards of directors.

471
00:22:14,780 --> 00:22:17,780
So, this is maybe the most essential kind of regulation.

472
00:22:17,780 --> 00:22:20,780
And it's within firm.

473
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:22,780
So, if you ever, and you

474
00:22:22,780 --> 00:22:24,780
may someday be serving on a board,

475
00:22:24,780 --> 00:22:26,780
the thing you have to remember

476
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:28,780
is that a board member has,

477
00:22:28,780 --> 00:22:30,780
if you ever agree to serve on a board,

478
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:33,780
I think you should interpret it as

479
00:22:33,780 --> 00:22:36,780
a moral obligation that you have taken on

480
00:22:36,780 --> 00:22:38,780
to prevent all of these things.

481
00:22:38,780 --> 00:22:39,780
Okay?

482
00:22:39,780 --> 00:22:41,280
And to make sure that the

483
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:42,780
business is run in a high-minded way.

484
00:22:42,780 --> 00:22:46,780
And remember that your reputation is at stake,

485
00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:48,780
because you're part of the company.

486
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:49,780
But it's more than that.

487
00:22:49,780 --> 00:22:50,780
It's a moral duty.

488
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:53,280
So, you have what's called the

489
00:22:53,280 --> 00:22:55,780
duty of care as a board member.

490
00:22:55,780 --> 00:22:59,780
Duty of care is a duty of care.

491
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:03,280
Duty of care is a duty to

492
00:23:03,280 --> 00:23:06,780
act as a reasonable, prudent, rational person would.

493
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:08,780
That means you have to make

494
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:10,780
sure that you're getting the information,

495
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:13,280
you're watching, you're being careful about

496
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:15,780
your obligation as a board member.

497
00:23:15,780 --> 00:23:18,780
You are not managing the company.

498
00:23:18,780 --> 00:23:21,780
You are watching the management of the company.

499
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:23,780
And you don't follow every detail,

500
00:23:23,780 --> 00:23:25,780
but you have to follow enough that

501
00:23:25,780 --> 00:23:27,780
you act as a sort of regulator.

502
00:23:27,780 --> 00:23:29,780
And you have to know what you're doing.

503
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:37,780
You also have a duty of loyalty.

504
00:23:37,780 --> 00:23:40,280
And that's usually interpreted

505
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:42,780
as loyalty to the shareholders.

506
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:46,780
I would say it's expanding.

507
00:23:46,780 --> 00:23:49,280
Now, there's more and more

508
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:51,780
talk of corporate social responsibility

509
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:54,780
and the loyalties that you have to other people,

510
00:23:54,780 --> 00:23:57,780
members of the community at large.

511
00:23:57,780 --> 00:23:59,780
But I'd say that, let's just start from the beginning.

512
00:23:59,780 --> 00:24:02,780
Because there's so much temptation to steal money,

513
00:24:02,780 --> 00:24:05,780
to tunnel money out of corporations,

514
00:24:05,780 --> 00:24:09,780
it probably remains true that your main duty of loyalty

515
00:24:09,780 --> 00:24:12,780
is to the actual shareholders.

516
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:15,780
Because they're the ones who put up the money

517
00:24:15,780 --> 00:24:20,780
and they're expecting to get a profit back.

518
00:24:20,780 --> 00:24:27,780
So, if you ever serve on the board of directors,

519
00:24:27,780 --> 00:24:30,780
I think you may become, it may seem,

520
00:24:30,780 --> 00:24:32,280
many board members don't view

521
00:24:32,280 --> 00:24:33,780
it as a very significant thing.

522
00:24:33,780 --> 00:24:35,280
They think, well, it's just an

523
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:36,780
honor to be on a board

524
00:24:36,780 --> 00:24:39,780
and I just show up at four meetings a year.

525
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:41,780
And we listen to a presentation

526
00:24:41,780 --> 00:24:43,780
from the CEO and we say, fine.

527
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:51,780
That's not what you should be doing.

528
00:24:51,780 --> 00:24:54,780
I think one has to kind of notice the ambiance.

529
00:24:54,780 --> 00:24:56,280
You have to use your

530
00:24:56,280 --> 00:24:57,780
intuitive judgment as a board member.

531
00:24:57,780 --> 00:25:04,780
About, is this an up and up firm?

532
00:25:04,780 --> 00:25:14,780
Is there a good atmosphere here?

533
00:25:14,780 --> 00:25:16,780
Anyway, I wanted to move up.

534
00:25:16,780 --> 00:25:23,780
So, that was my first thing at the firm level.

535
00:25:23,780 --> 00:25:25,780
Of course, firms have compliance departments.

536
00:25:25,780 --> 00:25:27,780
They have, you know,

537
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:32,780
they issue rules, they issue statements of purpose.

538
00:25:32,780 --> 00:25:36,780
And that's all within firm.

539
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:38,780
But I wanted to move up now.

540
00:25:38,780 --> 00:25:41,780
Another step in my regulatory thing.

541
00:25:41,780 --> 00:25:44,780
I moved to trade groups.

542
00:25:44,780 --> 00:25:50,780
And these are groups of firms that get together

543
00:25:50,780 --> 00:25:54,780
to form an organization.

544
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:00,780
So, well, what did I say?

545
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:05,780
Indra Nooyi was on the U.S. India, what was that?

546
00:26:05,780 --> 00:26:07,780
