00:39:30,280 --> 00:39:33,780
food safety and other business laws.

820
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:36,780
But regarding finance, it was the Blue Sky Laws.

821
00:39:36,780 --> 00:39:38,780
Now, I don't know if anyone

822
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:40,780
remembers why they're called Blue Sky Laws.

823
00:39:40,780 --> 00:39:43,280
But one interpretation is that there

824
00:39:43,280 --> 00:39:45,780
were a lot of salespeople for investments.

825
00:39:45,780 --> 00:39:48,280
And some of them would

826
00:39:48,280 --> 00:39:50,780
give you an exaggerated sales job.

827
00:39:50,780 --> 00:39:54,280
They would say, there's no limit to how

828
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:57,780
high the price can go of this stock.

829
00:39:57,780 --> 00:39:59,780
Nothing but the blue sky above.

830
00:39:59,780 --> 00:40:01,780
So, that's my story about how they

831
00:40:01,780 --> 00:40:03,780
got to be called Blue Sky Laws.

832
00:40:03,780 --> 00:40:04,780
But I don't know if there's any

833
00:40:04,780 --> 00:40:05,780
agreement on where that name came from.

834
00:40:06,780 --> 00:40:08,780
But what companies start, what

835
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:10,780
state governments started to do.

836
00:40:10,780 --> 00:40:16,780
I think the first Blue Sky Law

837
00:40:16,780 --> 00:40:22,780
was in the state of Kansas in 1911.

838
00:40:22,780 --> 00:40:29,280
It was given some impetus by a book

839
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:35,780
written by William Brandeis called Other People's Money.

840
00:40:36,780 --> 00:40:37,780
It was a book that was published in 1915.

841
00:40:37,780 --> 00:40:38,780
I think it was.

842
00:40:38,780 --> 00:40:45,780
That was a book that

843
00:40:45,780 --> 00:40:52,780
detailed the scandals of fake securities.

844
00:40:52,780 --> 00:40:54,780
Dishonest properties of securities.

845
00:40:54,780 --> 00:40:56,780
And the reason for that was,

846
00:40:56,780 --> 00:40:58,780
because of the fact that the

847
00:40:58,780 --> 00:41:00,780
state government was not a state government.

848
00:41:00,780 --> 00:41:02,780
And the state government was not a state government.

849
00:41:02,780 --> 00:41:04,780
So, they were not a state government.

850
00:41:04,780 --> 00:41:05,780
So, they were not a state government.

851
00:41:05,780 --> 00:41:09,780
They were just promoters of securities.

852
00:41:09,780 --> 00:41:11,780
So, it started in Kansas in 1911.

853
00:41:11,780 --> 00:41:14,780
But by the 1930s, practically

854
00:41:14,780 --> 00:41:17,780
every state had Blue Sky Laws.

855
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:23,780
And they had state regulators that enforced them.

856
00:41:23,780 --> 00:41:27,780
But the problem was that they were state laws.

857
00:41:27,780 --> 00:41:30,780
And it made it difficult for state governments.

858
00:41:30,780 --> 00:41:32,780
There's all these different state governments

859
00:41:32,780 --> 00:41:34,780
that are trying to regulate an industry,

860
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:36,780
and they're trying to regulate a state government.

861
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:38,780
And the problem is that the state

862
00:41:38,780 --> 00:41:40,780
government is really national or international in scope.

863
00:41:40,780 --> 00:41:42,780
So, people could evade the Blue Sky Laws.

864
00:41:42,780 --> 00:41:44,780
Now, especially since the telephone

865
00:41:44,780 --> 00:41:46,780
became prominent in the 1920s,

866
00:41:46,780 --> 00:41:48,280
you could set up a

867
00:41:48,280 --> 00:41:49,780
boiler room, so-called, in one state.

868
00:41:49,780 --> 00:41:52,780
A boiler room, I think I said this, is a,

869
00:41:52,780 --> 00:41:56,780
you rent the cheapest space for your telephone bank,

870
00:41:56,780 --> 00:41:58,780
and you pick the boiler room of some,

871
00:41:58,780 --> 00:42:00,780
in the basement of some building,

872
00:42:00,780 --> 00:42:02,780
and you put a bunch of people manning the telephones.

873
00:42:02,780 --> 00:42:04,780
And they call across state lines.

874
00:42:04,780 --> 00:42:07,780
So, this confuses the Blue Sky regulators,

875
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:10,780
because it involves two different states.

876
00:42:10,780 --> 00:42:12,780
And you call long distance to the other state,

877
00:42:12,780 --> 00:42:14,780
and you give, this used to happen a lot.

878
00:42:14,780 --> 00:42:16,780
It doesn't happen so much anymore.

879
00:42:16,780 --> 00:42:18,780
They would try to sell you on some,

880
00:42:18,780 --> 00:42:20,780
I've got a great stock, it's

881
00:42:20,780 --> 00:42:22,780
going to triple in five days.

882
00:42:22,780 --> 00:42:25,780
If you don't buy it today, it's too late.

883
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:27,280
And they would get people

884
00:42:27,280 --> 00:42:28,780
into stocks that were utterly worthless.

885
00:42:28,780 --> 00:42:32,780
And it was fraud.

886
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:34,780
And so, that made it,

887
00:42:34,780 --> 00:42:41,780
the whole progressive movement to regulate securities

888
00:42:41,780 --> 00:42:43,780
was of limited success.

889
00:42:43,780 --> 00:42:46,780
But it left in place a

890
00:42:46,780 --> 00:42:49,780
huge state and local regulatory system

891
00:42:49,780 --> 00:42:52,780
that was considered inadequate to the task,

892
00:42:52,780 --> 00:42:55,780
but which remains today

893
00:42:55,780 --> 00:42:59,780
for the regulation of smaller companies.

894
00:42:59,780 --> 00:43:02,780
The next step is the national regulation.

895
00:43:02,780 --> 00:43:05,780
The national regulation is the regulation

896
00:43:05,780 --> 00:43:07,780
of the United States,

897
00:43:07,780 --> 00:43:10,780
which is the regulation of the United States

898
00:43:10,780 --> 00:43:14,780
under the new deal of President Roosevelt.

899
00:43:14,780 --> 00:43:17,780
So, for national.

900
00:43:17,780 --> 00:43:23,780
Notably, in 1934, I mentioned this before,

901
00:43:23,780 --> 00:43:26,780
the U.S. government set up the,

902
00:43:26,780 --> 00:43:28,780
actually, let me spell it out,

903
00:43:28,780 --> 00:43:31,780
securities and,

904
00:43:31,780 --> 00:43:35,780
exchange commission.

905
00:43:41,780 --> 00:43:43,780
Or SEC.

906
00:43:43,780 --> 00:43:47,780
Which regulates the larger firm.

907
00:43:47,780 --> 00:43:51,780
Practically, any firm that's listed on a stock exchange

908
00:43:51,780 --> 00:43:53,780
would be regulated by them.

909
00:43:53,780 --> 00:43:57,780
They,

910
00:43:57,780 --> 00:44:00,780
one of the,

911
00:44:00,780 --> 00:44:05,280
one of the first directors of

912
00:44:05,280 --> 00:44:09,780
the SEC was William O. Douglas,

913
00:44:09,780 --> 00:44:12,780
a Yale Law professor,

914
00:44:12,780 --> 00:44:16,780
who wrote a book

915
00:44:16,780 --> 00:44:19,780
called Democracy and Finance,

916
00:44:19,780 --> 00:44:23,780
about his experience as SEC director.

917
00:44:23,780 --> 00:44:28,780
And he talked about the,

918
00:44:30,780 --> 00:44:34,780
the conflicts he had with Wall Street at the time.

919
00:44:34,780 --> 00:44:37,780
They really didn't like this regulation.

920
00:44:37,780 --> 00:44:40,780
It was an antagonistic atmosphere.

921
00:44:40,780 --> 00:44:43,780
The Securities and Exchange Commission

922
00:44:43,780 --> 00:44:46,780
was widely regarded in the business community

923
00:44:46,780 --> 00:44:49,780
as practically a socialist organization.

924
00:44:49,780 --> 00:44:52,780
And that's a dirty word in the United States.

925
00:44:52,780 --> 00:44:55,780
Because it seemed like the government was involving itself

926
00:44:55,780 --> 00:44:57,280
in things that they had

927
00:44:57,280 --> 00:44:58,780
no business in involving themselves in.

928
00:44:58,780 --> 00:45:03,780
So, Douglas fought kind of a battle against Wall Street,

929
00:45:03,780 --> 00:45:05,780
as detailed in his book.

930
00:45:05,780 --> 00:45:09,780
But to get everything on an up and up format,

931
00:45:09,780 --> 00:45:12,780
he was following one of the rules that Brandeis,

932
00:45:12,780 --> 00:45:17,780
the thing is, he wanted disclosure.

933
00:45:17,780 --> 00:45:21,780
Louis Brandeis, there's a famous quote in his book,

934
00:45:21,780 --> 00:45:23,780
Other People's Money.

935
00:45:23,780 --> 00:45:24,780
And it is, quote,

936
00:45:24,780 --> 00:45:27,780
sunshine is the best disinfectant.

937
00:45:27,780 --> 00:45:30,780
That's in a parable to hanging out your clothes

938
00:45:30,780 --> 00:45:33,780
on the clothesline and letting the sunlight fall on them

939
00:45:33,780 --> 00:45:36,780
and that disinfects them.

940
00:45:36,780 --> 00:45:39,780
But the same idea applies in finance.

941
00:45:39,780 --> 00:45:42,780
If everybody knows what the firm is doing,

942
00:45:42,780 --> 00:45:45,780
they'll figure it out and there'll be talk.

943
00:45:45,780 --> 00:45:50,780
We just don't want firms to keep secrets.

944
00:45:50,780 --> 00:45:54,780
So, the SEC is built around disclosure.

945
00:45:54,780 --> 00:45:56,780
And so,

946
00:45:56,780 --> 00:45:58,780
I mentioned,

947
00:45:58,780 --> 00:46:05,780
Edgar is their online information system,

948
00:46:05,780 --> 00:46:08,780
but it's on sec.gov.

949
00:46:08,780 --> 00:46:11,780
They put everything up about a company,

950
00:46:11,780 --> 00:46:14,780
that is, of any public company,

951
00:46:14,780 --> 00:46:16,780
up on that website.

952
00:46:16,780 --> 00:46:19,780
And it's free, absolutely free, to the world.

953
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:21,780
That's disclosure.

954
00:46:21,780 --> 00:46:24,780
And that was the motivating thing.

955
00:46:24,780 --> 00:46:25,780
Another,

956
00:46:25,780 --> 00:46:28,780
SEC chairman,

957
00:46:28,780 --> 00:46:34,780
SEC chairman was Arthur Levitt.

958
00:46:34,780 --> 00:46:38,780
And he wrote a book called Take On the Street

959
00:46:38,780 --> 00:46:42,780
after he left SEC chairmanship.

960
00:46:42,780 --> 00:46:44,780
This was recently,

961
00:46:44,780 --> 00:46:47,780
I mean, like in the last 10 years.

962
00:46:47,780 --> 00:46:50,780
And he told a lot of,

963
00:46:50,780 --> 00:46:54,780
a lot of stories about nasty people

964
00:46:54,780 --> 00:46:56,780
on Wall Street.

965
00:46:56,780 --> 00:46:59,780
He said he had vivid memories

966
00:46:59,780 --> 00:47:01,780
of the reactions he got

967
00:47:01,780 --> 00:47:04,780
from proposed new regulations.

968
00:47:04,780 --> 00:47:06,780
And,

969
00:47:06,780 --> 00:47:10,780
he was particularly upset by the kind of ability

970
00:47:10,780 --> 00:47:13,780
that Wall Street had to fool people by

971
00:47:13,780 --> 00:47:16,780
using complex language that they couldn't understand.

972
00:47:16,780 --> 00:47:19,780
They would write prospectuses that no one could understand

973
00:47:19,780 --> 00:47:21,780
without a law degree.

974
00:47:21,780 --> 00:47:23,780
He wanted plain English.

975
00:47:23,780 --> 00:47:25,780
So, again,

976
00:47:25,780 --> 00:47:28,780
taking a job as SEC chairman

977
00:47:28,780 --> 00:47:30,780
or a commissioner on the SEC

978
00:47:30,780 --> 00:47:34,780
is taking on a high,

979
00:47:34,780 --> 00:47:38,780
high tension job, I think.

980
00:47:38,780 --> 00:47:39,780
But it's all,

981
00:47:39,780 --> 00:47:41,780
it's like the same thing as in sports.

982
00:47:41,780 --> 00:47:43,780
You know how referees in sports

983
00:47:43,780 --> 00:47:47,780
get punched every now and then by the athletes.

984
00:47:47,780 --> 00:47:51,780
But we all see that they're necessary.

985
00:47:53,780 --> 00:47:56,780
So,

986
00:47:56,780 --> 00:47:58,780
one thing that the SEC does

987
00:47:58,780 --> 00:48:00,780
is protects small investors

988
00:48:00,780 --> 00:48:03,780
by managing the distinction,

989
00:48:03,780 --> 00:48:04,780
I mentioned this before,

990
00:48:04,780 --> 00:48:07,780
between public and private securities.

991
00:48:07,780 --> 00:48:11,780
So,

992
00:48:11,780 --> 00:48:13,780
if you want to

993
00:48:13,780 --> 00:48:14,780
go public

994
00:48:14,780 --> 00:48:16,780
and list your security,

995
00:48:16,780 --> 00:48:18,780
the shares of your company

996
00:48:18,780 --> 00:48:20,780
on a stock exchange,

997
00:48:20,780 --> 00:48:22,780
you have to go through a procedure

998
00:48:22,780 --> 00:48:25,780
defined by the SEC

999
00:48:25,780 --> 00:48:28,780
called an IPO.

1000
00:48:28,780 --> 00:48:32,780
That stands for Initial Public Offering.

1001
00:48:32,780 --> 00:48:34,780
And to do that procedure,

1002
00:48:34,780 --> 00:48:37,780
you have to follow a system of rules

1003
00:48:37,780 --> 00:48:40,780
dictated by the SEC

1004
00:48:40,780 --> 00:48:44,780
that makes it difficult for you to do any shenanigans,

1005
00:48:44,780 --> 00:48:46,780
any tricks.

1006
00:48:46,780 --> 00:48:49,780
It creates a level playing field.

1007
00:48:49,780 --> 00:48:51,780
And the rules are very strict.

1008
00:48:52,780 --> 00:48:56,780
And enforced by the SEC.

1009
00:48:56,780 --> 00:49:00,780
The SEC allows other companies

1010
00:49:00,780 --> 00:49:03,780
to remain private.

1011
00:49:03,780 --> 00:49:07,780
But it has rules about what you have to do

1012
00:49:07,780 --> 00:49:09,780
to stay private.

1013
00:49:09,780 --> 00:49:11,780
Public,

1014
00:49:11,780 --> 00:49:13,780
when you become a public company,

1015
00:49:13,780 --> 00:49:16,780
you are involved in the public trust.

1016
00:49:16,780 --> 00:49:21,780
And you have to,

1017
00:49:21,780 --> 00:49:23,780
among other things,

1018
00:49:23,780 --> 00:49:27,780
include all of your documents on SEC.gov.

1019
00:49:27,780 --> 00:49:31,780
But they do allow private companies.

1020
00:49:31,780 --> 00:49:34,780
And a particularly important category

1021
00:49:34,780 --> 00:49:36,780
of private company

1022
00:49:36,780 --> 00:49:41,780
is the so-called hedge fund.

1023
00:49:41,780 --> 00:49:43,780
A hedge fund

1024
00:49:43,780 --> 00:49:46,780
is an investment company

1025
00:49:46,780 --> 00:49:50,780
for wealthy individuals only.

1026
00:49:51,780 --> 00:49:54,780
And there's,

1027
00:49:54,780 --> 00:49:56,780
the idea is that

1028
00:49:56,780 --> 00:49:58,780
small investors need to be protected.

1029
00:49:58,780 --> 00:50:00,780
Because

1030
00:50:00,780 --> 00:50:01,780
they don't know,

1031
00:50:01,780 --> 00:50:02,780
they can't,

1032
00:50:02,780 --> 00:50:04,780
they don't have expensive advisors

1033
00:50:04,780 --> 00:50:06,780
and lawyers to tell them what to do.

1034
00:50:06,780 --> 00:50:09,780
And so,

1035
00:50:09,780 --> 00:50:12,780
lots of controversial things can't be done

1036
00:50:12,780 --> 00:50:16,780
in funds that are offered to the public.

1037
00:50:16,780 --> 00:50:18,780
A hedge fund

1038
00:50:18,780 --> 00:50:21,780
is a fund for these wealthy investors

1039
00:50:21,780 --> 00:50:24,780
and the SEC

1040
00:50:24,780 --> 00:50:27,780
has minimal regulation of them.

1041
00:50:27,780 --> 00:50:29,780
The Dodd-Frank bill,

1042
00:50:29,780 --> 00:50:30,780
we thought,

1043
00:50:30,780 --> 00:50:32,780
would put more regulation on them.

1044
00:50:32,780 --> 00:50:34,780
But they're still surviving

1045
00:50:34,780 --> 00:50:38,780
as largely unregulated organizations.

1046
00:50:38,780 --> 00:50:43,780
They have different kinds of,

1047
00:50:43,780 --> 00:50:45,780
there's different,

1048
00:50:45,780 --> 00:50:46,780
the law,

1049
00:50:46,780 --> 00:50:48,780
the SEC code of rules is huge

1050
00:50:48,780 --> 00:50:49,780
and it's complicated.

1051
00:50:49,780 --> 00:50:50,780
I guess you can read about it

1052
00:50:50,780 --> 00:50:53,780
on the SEC.gov.

1053
00:50:53,780 --> 00:50:58,780
But there's a 3C1S hedge fund.

1054
00:50:58,780 --> 00:51:00,780
There's one type of hedge fund.

1055
00:51:00,780 --> 00:51:04,780
It can take on no more than 99 investors.

1056
00:51:04,780 --> 00:51:07,780
Can't be more than 100.

1057
00:51:07,780 --> 00:51:13,780
And they must be accredited investors.

1058
00:51:13,780 --> 00:51:18,780
And the SEC defines accredited investors.

1059
00:51:18,780 --> 00:51:19,780
That is,

1060
00:51:19,780 --> 00:51:23,780
they were proposing to change the definition,

1061
00:51:23,780 --> 00:51:25,780
but I think it's still stuck.

1062
00:51:25,780 --> 00:51:27,780
To be an accredited investor,

1063
00:51:27,780 --> 00:51:29,780
I don't have it here.

1064
00:51:29,780 --> 00:51:32,780
I think it's still that you had to

1065
00:51:32,780 --> 00:51:34,780
have an income of

1066
00:51:34,780 --> 00:51:36,780
at least 200,000 a year

1067
00:51:36,780 --> 00:51:37,780
if you're single

1068
00:51:37,780 --> 00:51:39,780
or 300,000 if you're married.

1069
00:51:39,780 --> 00:51:44,780
Or $1 million in investable assets,

1070
00:51:44,780 --> 00:51:46,780
not including your house.

1071
00:51:46,780 --> 00:51:47,780
I think that's the definition.

1072
00:51:47,780 --> 00:51:48,780
I've got it fairly close.

1073
00:51:49,780 --> 00:51:53,780
So, it excludes most investors.

1074
00:51:53,780 --> 00:51:55,780
But then there's other kinds of hedge funds.

1075
00:51:55,780 --> 00:52:01,780
There's also a 3C7S hedge fund

1076
00:52:01,780 --> 00:52:10,780
that's allowed to take on 500 investors.

1077
00:52:10,780 --> 00:52:14,780
But these have to have

1078
00:52:14,780 --> 00:52:18,780
a net worth of $5 million.

1079
00:52:18,780 --> 00:52:20,780
Or if they're an institution,

1080
00:52:20,780 --> 00:52:22,780
it can't be a little institution.

1081
00:52:22,780 --> 00:52:25,780
So, it would be 5 million.

1082
00:52:25,780 --> 00:52:27,780
So, it's more than accredited investor.

1083
00:52:27,780 --> 00:52:29,780
It's called super accredited.

1084
00:52:29,780 --> 00:52:32,780
5 million for individuals,

1085
00:52:32,780 --> 00:52:35,780
25 million for institutions.

1086
00:52:35,780 --> 00:52:42,780
That excludes most family foundations, I suspect.

1087
00:52:42,780 --> 00:52:45,780
So,

1088
00:52:45,780 --> 00:52:46,780
hedge funds,

1089
00:52:46,780 --> 00:52:48,780
you don't hear about as much,

1090
00:52:48,780 --> 00:52:50,780
because they don't advertise.

1091
00:52:50,780 --> 00:52:53,780
And they are not allowed to advertise.

1092
00:52:53,780 --> 00:52:55,780
If you click on their website,

1093
00:52:55,780 --> 00:52:56,780
you will find a very,

1094
00:52:56,780 --> 00:52:58,780
they'll have a website.

1095
00:52:58,780 --> 00:53:00,780
It will give you the contact information,

1096
00:53:00,780 --> 00:53:01,780
typically,

1097
00:53:01,780 --> 00:53:03,780
and say very little more about them.

1098
00:53:03,780 --> 00:53:06,780
They don't put anything on their website,

1099
00:53:06,780 --> 00:53:09,780
because they worry that they'd run afoul of the SEC,

1100
00:53:09,780 --> 00:53:11,780
that the SEC would call it advertising.

1101
00:53:11,780 --> 00:53:15,780
And you're not supposed to be for the public.

1102
00:53:15,780 --> 00:53:17,780
This creates some conflict.

1103
00:53:17,780 --> 00:53:19,780
And I think it's important for people to think,

1104
00:53:19,780 --> 00:53:21,780
why is it that the United States

1105
00:53:21,780 --> 00:53:24,780
has this kind of two-tier system?

1106
00:53:24,780 --> 00:53:25,780
It's not just the United States,

1107
00:53:25,780 --> 00:53:27,780
other countries have this as well.

1108
00:53:27,780 --> 00:53:29,780
But let's talk about this system.

1109
00:53:29,780 --> 00:53:31,780
Why is it that we

1110
00:53:31,780 --> 00:53:33,780
have special rules for poor people,

1111
00:53:33,780 --> 00:53:36,780
which in effect, in their estimation,

1112
00:53:36,780 --> 00:53:40,780
excludes them from some of the biggest profit opportunities.

1113
00:53:40,780 --> 00:53:42,780
Hedge funds are allowed to charge,

1114
00:53:42,780 --> 00:53:46,780
to impose high management fees.

1115
00:53:46,780 --> 00:53:49,780
And the highest management fee

1116
00:53:49,780 --> 00:53:52,780
has been called 2 and 20.

1117
00:53:55,780 --> 00:53:58,780
2% of the assets under management

1118
00:53:58,780 --> 00:54:01,780
goes to the managers every year.

1119
00:54:01,780 --> 00:54:05,780
And 20% of any trading profits they make

1120
00:54:05,780 --> 00:54:07,780
go to the managers.

1121
00:54:07,780 --> 00:54:09,780
Nothing of their trading losses.

1122
00:54:09,780 --> 00:54:10,780
If they lose money,

1123
00:54:10,780 --> 00:54:12,780
it doesn't come from the managers.

1124
00:54:12,780 --> 00:54:15,780
Now, that sounds like a really good deal, right?

1125
00:54:15,780 --> 00:54:16,780
Think about it.

1126
00:54:16,780 --> 00:54:18,780
If you lose just a billion dollars

1127
00:54:18,780 --> 00:54:20,780
in your management fund,

1128
00:54:20,780 --> 00:54:21,780
you're getting 2%.

1129
00:54:21,780 --> 00:54:23,780
What is 2% of a billion dollars?

1130
00:54:23,780 --> 00:54:24,780
That's 20 million.

1131
00:54:24,780 --> 00:54:26,780
Did I get that right?

1132
00:54:26,780 --> 00:54:28,780
And 20% of the profits.

1133
00:54:28,780 --> 00:54:30,280
I mean, you're going to make

1134
00:54:30,280 --> 00:54:31,780
a huge amount of money, right?

1135
00:54:31,780 --> 00:54:33,780
I suspect some of you will do this,

1136
00:54:33,780 --> 00:54:35,780
because it's so tempting.

1137
00:54:35,780 --> 00:54:37,780
But of course, you can't do this.

1138
00:54:37,780 --> 00:54:39,780
It's not so easy to start a hedge fund,

1139
00:54:39,780 --> 00:54:41,780
because the whole idea of a hedge fund

1140
00:54:41,780 --> 00:54:42,780
in most people's minds is,

1141
00:54:42,780 --> 00:54:44,780
there are genius traders.

1142
00:54:44,780 --> 00:54:45,780
And you got to pay them, right?

1143
00:54:45,780 --> 00:54:49,780
You can't just hire them cheap.

1144
00:54:49,780 --> 00:54:52,780
So, the hedge funds raid all of the geniuses

1145
00:54:52,780 --> 00:54:53,780
out of the mutual funds,

1146
00:54:53,780 --> 00:54:55,780
which are public investment companies

1147
00:54:55,780 --> 00:54:56,780
for these retail investors.

1148
00:54:56,780 --> 00:54:59,780
They get the smartest guys

1149
00:54:59,780 --> 00:55:01,780
and pay them this huge amount.

1150
00:55:01,780 --> 00:55:03,780
And the idea is,

1151
00:55:03,780 --> 00:55:05,780
they'll make a lot of money.

1152
00:55:05,780 --> 00:55:08,780
Whether they really do make a lot of money

1153
00:55:08,780 --> 00:55:11,780
is a difficult question.

1154
00:55:11,780 --> 00:55:13,780
It's hard to tell,

1155
00:55:13,780 --> 00:55:14,780
because hedge funds haven't been

1156
00:55:14,780 --> 00:55:18,780
around, they're changing through time.

1157
00:55:18,780 --> 00:55:20,280
I'm sure some of them

1158
00:55:20,280 --> 00:55:21,780
do make a lot of money.

1159
00:55:21,780 --> 00:55:25,780
Some of them are good investments.

1160
00:55:25,780 --> 00:55:27,780
But it's like a wild west.

1161
00:55:27,780 --> 00:55:29,780
It's a jungle out there.

1162
00:55:29,780 --> 00:55:31,780
And the SEC doesn't think that you,

1163
00:55:31,780 --> 00:55:32,780
the small investor,

1164
00:55:32,780 --> 00:55:34,780
should get involved in that.

1165
00:55:34,780 --> 00:55:36,780
And so, you won't.

1166
00:55:36,780 --> 00:55:38,780
That's another example.

1167
00:55:38,780 --> 00:55:42,780
So, market surveying,

1168
00:55:42,780 --> 00:55:45,780
market surveillance is something

1169
00:55:45,780 --> 00:55:47,780
that the SEC does.

1170
00:55:47,780 --> 00:55:49,780
And it does it in connection

1171
00:55:49,780 --> 00:55:53,780
with the self-regulatory organizations,

1172
00:55:53,780 --> 00:55:56,780
like the stock exchanges.

1173
00:55:56,780 --> 00:55:59,780
It's again trying to prevent manipulative behavior.

1174
00:55:59,780 --> 00:56:07,780
So, I'll give you an example of market,

1175
00:56:07,780 --> 00:56:10,780
this is my,

1176
00:56:12,780 --> 00:56:15,780
see the markets don't function that well by themselves

1177
00:56:15,780 --> 00:56:18,780
without some kind of referee.

1178
00:56:18,780 --> 00:56:25,780
The 1792 stock market crash was only one example.

1179
00:56:25,780 --> 00:56:27,780
But market surveillance is now

1180
00:56:27,780 --> 00:56:29,780
a well-established principle to prevent

1181
00:56:29,780 --> 00:56:33,780
manipulators from taking over a market.

1182
00:56:33,780 --> 00:56:37,780
I'll give you an example of what happens.

1183
00:56:37,780 --> 00:56:41,780
This is a news story from 1995.

1184
00:56:41,780 --> 00:56:44,780
In May 1995,

1185
00:56:44,780 --> 00:56:47,780
a secretary at IBM Corporation

1186
00:56:47,780 --> 00:56:50,780
was asked to copy documents

1187
00:56:50,780 --> 00:56:52,780
related to secret plans

1188
00:56:52,780 --> 00:56:54,780
to take over the Lotus Corporation.

1189
00:56:54,780 --> 00:56:56,780
Okay, Lotus was the spreadsheet,

1190
00:56:56,780 --> 00:56:58,780
doesn't exist anymore,

1191
00:56:58,780 --> 00:57:00,780
spreadsheet company that

1192
00:57:00,780 --> 00:57:03,780
was a pioneer in computer spreadsheets.

1193
00:57:03,780 --> 00:57:06,780
So anyway, she just commented to her husband

1194
00:57:06,780 --> 00:57:08,780
because she was copying these documents.

1195
00:57:08,780 --> 00:57:09,780
She said,

1196
00:57:09,780 --> 00:57:12,780
oh, IBM was going to take over Lotus.

1197
00:57:12,780 --> 00:57:14,780
Her husband saw that

1198
00:57:14,780 --> 00:57:17,780
as valuable information, right?

1199
00:57:17,780 --> 00:57:20,780
The stock price of Lotus is likely to go up.

1200
00:57:20,780 --> 00:57:22,780
And she said they were going to do it

1201
00:57:22,780 --> 00:57:24,780
in three days.

1202
00:57:24,780 --> 00:57:26,780
So that's a perfect thing.

1203
00:57:26,780 --> 00:57:27,780
So he went,

1204
00:57:27,780 --> 00:57:28,780
well, anyway, what did he do?

1205
00:57:28,780 --> 00:57:31,780
He merely telephoned all of his friends

1206
00:57:31,780 --> 00:57:33,780
and told them,

1207
00:57:33,780 --> 00:57:35,780
by the takeover date,

1208
00:57:35,780 --> 00:57:37,780
25 people had bought

1209
00:57:37,780 --> 00:57:39,780
a half a million dollars

1210
00:57:39,780 --> 00:57:41,780
and they were going to take over Lotus.

1211
00:57:41,780 --> 00:57:43,780
These people included a pizza chef,

1212
00:57:43,780 --> 00:57:45,780
an electrical engineer,

1213
00:57:45,780 --> 00:57:46,780
a bank executive,

1214
00:57:46,780 --> 00:57:47,780
a dairy wholesaler,

1215
00:57:47,780 --> 00:57:49,780
a school teacher,

1216
00:57:49,780 --> 00:57:51,780
and four stockbrokers.

1217
00:57:51,780 --> 00:57:52,780
Okay.

1218
00:57:52,780 --> 00:57:54,780
Well, they caught them

1219
00:57:54,780 --> 00:57:56,780
because they saw this unusual activity

1220
00:57:56,780 --> 00:57:58,780
in the stock

1221
00:57:58,780 --> 00:58:01,780
and they

1222
00:58:01,780 --> 00:58:04,780
subpoenaed their phone records

1223
00:58:04,780 --> 00:58:06,780
and they figured out who called who

1224
00:58:06,780 --> 00:58:08,780
and they called them in and said,

1225
00:58:08,780 --> 00:58:09,780
well, I'm not going to tell you

1226
00:58:09,780 --> 00:58:11,780
what they've done.

1227
00:58:11,780 --> 00:58:13,780
And they were all punished for this.

1228
00:58:13,780 --> 00:58:14,780
It's illegal.

1229
00:58:14,780 --> 00:58:16,780
You can't trade on inside information.

1230
00:58:16,780 --> 00:58:18,780
Again, the SEC is trying to create

1231
00:58:18,780 --> 00:58:21,780
a level playing field for everyone.

1232
00:58:21,780 --> 00:58:26,780
I'll give you another example.

1233
00:58:26,780 --> 00:58:30,780
It was Emulex Corporation,

1234
00:58:30,780 --> 00:58:35,780
a former employee of this company,

1235
00:58:35,780 --> 00:58:37,780
shorted stock

1236
00:58:37,780 --> 00:58:40,780
in his own former company.

1237
00:58:40,780 --> 00:58:42,780
And he was,

1238
00:58:42,780 --> 00:58:44,780
then decided he would try to help make

1239
00:58:44,780 --> 00:58:47,780
the price go down.

1240
00:58:47,780 --> 00:58:49,780
So, he sent,

1241
00:58:49,780 --> 00:58:50,780
because he knew the company,

1242
00:58:50,780 --> 00:58:51,780
he knew how to do this.

1243
00:58:51,780 --> 00:58:54,780
He sent a fake press release

1244
00:58:54,780 --> 00:58:56,780
to the internet.

1245
00:58:56,780 --> 00:58:58,780
And nobody suspected it.

1246
00:58:58,780 --> 00:59:00,780
It looked real.

1247
00:59:00,780 --> 00:59:03,780
He just sent it out from his own computer.

1248
00:59:03,780 --> 00:59:05,780
Actually, it wasn't his own computer.

1249
00:59:05,780 --> 00:59:06,780
He thought he was being smart.

1250
00:59:06,780 --> 00:59:10,780
He went to the El Camino Community College Library.

1251
00:59:10,780 --> 00:59:13,780
You know this story.

1252
00:59:13,780 --> 00:59:15,780
Or you know the college.

1253
00:59:15,780 --> 00:59:17,780
Well, he went to the library

1254
00:59:17,780 --> 00:59:19,780
and he sent the press release

1255
00:59:19,780 --> 00:59:21,780
from the library's computer.

1256
00:59:21,780 --> 00:59:23,780
And he thought, that's going to be, sure.

1257
00:59:23,780 --> 00:59:25,780
And then he,

1258
00:59:25,780 --> 00:59:26,780
it worked.

1259
00:59:26,780 --> 00:59:28,780
It was picked up by all the news services,

1260
00:59:28,780 --> 00:59:30,780
the bad news about Emulex.

1261
00:59:30,780 --> 00:59:32,780
And then he immediately sold,

1262
00:59:32,780 --> 00:59:34,780
recovered his short position.

1263
00:59:34,780 --> 00:59:35,780
And he made a big profit.

1264
00:59:35,780 --> 00:59:37,780
And he thought he was smart.

1265
00:59:37,780 --> 00:59:41,780
But he wasn't smart enough for the surveillance.

1266
00:59:41,780 --> 00:59:45,780
Because they suspected,

1267
00:59:45,780 --> 00:59:47,780
well, immediately,

1268
00:59:47,780 --> 00:59:50,780
it was Emulex immediately protested.

1269
00:59:50,780 --> 00:59:52,780
We didn't send out this press release.

1270
00:59:52,780 --> 00:59:56,780
So, the market surveillance team went into action.

1271
00:59:56,780 --> 00:59:58,780
And they found out,

1272
00:59:58,780 --> 01:00:00,780
it's all in the Stock Exchange record,

1273
01:00:00,780 --> 01:00:01,780
who covered a short position

1274
01:00:01,780 --> 01:00:03,780
right after the announcement.

1275
01:00:03,780 --> 01:00:04,780
They got his name,

1276
01:00:04,780 --> 01:00:06,780
as one of the people.

1277
01:00:06,780 --> 01:00:08,780
They actually traced down

1278
01:00:08,780 --> 01:00:11,780
where the email was sent from.

1279
01:00:11,780 --> 01:00:14,780
And they went to El Camino Community College.

1280
01:00:14,780 --> 01:00:16,780
And they talked to the librarians.

1281
01:00:16,780 --> 01:00:18,780
And they showed them photographs.

1282
01:00:18,780 --> 01:00:20,780
And they remembered him.

1283
01:00:20,780 --> 01:00:22,780
And so, they nailed this guy.

1284
01:00:22,780 --> 01:00:26,780
So, that's an example of what happens in our markets.

1285
01:00:26,780 --> 01:00:29,780
And so, that makes things work well.

1286
01:00:29,780 --> 01:00:34,780
So, in this category,

1287
01:00:34,780 --> 01:00:37,780
I also wanted to mention

1288
01:00:37,780 --> 01:00:42,780
what the SEC does with creating private organizations,

1289
01:00:42,780 --> 01:00:45,780
or self-regulatory organizations,

1290
01:00:45,780 --> 01:00:47,780
that do some of its jobs.

1291
01:00:47,780 --> 01:00:53,780
So, we have something called FASB,

1292
01:00:53,780 --> 01:00:57,780
which is called the Financial Accounting Standards Board.

1293
01:00:57,780 --> 01:00:59,780
It's right here in Connecticut.

1294
01:00:59,780 --> 01:01:03,780
The SEC decided that companies

1295
01:01:03,780 --> 01:01:06,780
fake their books in too many ways.

1296
01:01:06,780 --> 01:01:09,780
There's too much funny business going on.

1297
01:01:09,780 --> 01:01:12,780
And we need to manage this better.

1298
01:01:12,780 --> 01:01:15,780
But they didn't want to take over completely

1299
01:01:15,780 --> 01:01:17,780
this as a government function.

1300
01:01:17,780 --> 01:01:28,780
So, the government recognized

1301
01:01:28,780 --> 01:01:32,780
an industry group called FASB

1302
01:01:32,780 --> 01:01:35,780
as the arbiter of accounting standards.

1303
01:01:35,780 --> 01:01:38,780
And FASB is the authority

1304
01:01:38,780 --> 01:01:42,780
that defines GAP accounting standards.

1305
01:01:42,780 --> 01:01:45,780
And GAP is an acronym

1306
01:01:45,780 --> 01:01:50,780
for Generally Accepted Accounting Practices.

1307
01:01:50,780 --> 01:01:52,780
I'm sorry, principles.

1308
01:01:52,780 --> 01:01:56,780
Generally Accepted Accounting Principles

1309
01:01:56,780 --> 01:01:58,780
are defined by a private manager.

1310
01:01:58,780 --> 01:02:00,780
It's a private group.

1311
01:02:00,780 --> 01:02:03,780
And these principles are used on EDGAR,

1312
01:02:03,780 --> 01:02:06,780
which is the website that the SEC

1313
01:02:06,780 --> 01:02:09,780
uses to present accounting.

1314
01:02:09,780 --> 01:02:11,780
So, they're in Norwalk, Connecticut,

1315
01:02:11,780 --> 01:02:13,780
and they have their own website.

1316
01:02:13,780 --> 01:02:20,780
And you can see how they define accounting standards.

1317
01:02:20,780 --> 01:02:22,780
I'm running out of time here.

1318
01:02:22,780 --> 01:02:26,780
I wanted to tell you about

1319
01:02:26,780 --> 01:02:31,780
a government agency called SIPC.

1320
01:02:31,780 --> 01:02:33,780
I'm just having too much fun

1321
01:02:33,780 --> 01:02:36,780
telling you about all these.

1322
01:02:36,780 --> 01:02:43,780
SIPC is the Securities Investor Protection Corporation,

1323
01:02:43,780 --> 01:02:50,780
created by the United States Congress in 1970.

1324
01:02:50,780 --> 01:02:54,780
And it's part of an effort to protect small investors.

1325
01:02:54,780 --> 01:02:59,780
The SIPC ensures your brokerage accounts

1326
01:02:59,780 --> 01:03:04,780
against losses due to failure of your stockbroker.

1327
01:03:04,780 --> 01:03:11,780
It corresponds to the FDIC.

1328
01:03:11,780 --> 01:03:13,780
Did I mention this before?

1329
01:03:13,780 --> 01:03:15,780
Maybe I mentioned it briefly.

1330
01:03:15,780 --> 01:03:20,780
The FDIC is the company that insures your bank account.

1331
01:03:20,780 --> 01:03:23,780
SIPC is the company that insures your brokerage account.

1332
01:03:23,780 --> 01:03:27,780
And it's a government corporation.

1333
01:03:27,780 --> 01:03:35,780
It has limits on the amount that it insures.

1334
01:03:35,780 --> 01:03:39,780
It was actually created in 1970 in response.

1335
01:03:39,780 --> 01:03:42,780
A lot of things happen in response to a crisis.

1336
01:03:42,780 --> 01:03:47,780
There was a brokerage firm called Good Body

1337
01:03:47,780 --> 01:03:50,780
that failed in 1970,

1338
01:03:50,780 --> 01:03:52,780
just before SIPC was created.

1339
01:03:52,780 --> 01:03:55,780
A lot of people had their stocks in Good Body,

1340
01:03:55,780 --> 01:03:58,780
and they had accounts there.

1341
01:03:58,780 --> 01:04:00,780
And it looked like they were going to lose it.

1342
01:04:00,780 --> 01:04:02,780
They held their stocks in street name.

1343
01:04:02,780 --> 01:04:04,780
I remember telling you about that.

1344
01:04:04,780 --> 01:04:05,780
So, they thought they owned shares,

1345
01:04:05,780 --> 01:04:07,780
but really Good Body owned the shares.

1346
01:04:07,780 --> 01:04:09,780
Or they thought Good Body owned the shares,

1347
01:04:09,780 --> 01:04:11,780
but Good Body went under.

1348
01:04:11,780 --> 01:04:14,280
They ran the risk of losing

1349
01:04:14,280 --> 01:04:16,780
the shares they thought they owned.

1350
01:04:16,780 --> 01:04:21,780
So, SIPC was created in order to prevent that.

1351
01:04:22,780 --> 01:04:24,780
And it's a good thing.

1352
01:04:24,780 --> 01:04:27,780
Continuing along on the national level,

1353
01:04:27,780 --> 01:04:32,780
regulation is getting stronger at the national level.

1354
01:04:32,780 --> 01:04:34,780
I'll talk about two examples.

1355
01:04:34,780 --> 01:04:36,280
The U.S. and I'm going

1356
01:04:36,280 --> 01:04:37,780
to talk about the European Union

1357
01:04:37,780 --> 01:04:39,780
as if it were a nation.

1358
01:04:39,780 --> 01:04:41,780
It's sort of a nation.

1359
01:04:41,780 --> 01:04:43,780
It's an assembly of nations.

1360
01:04:43,780 --> 01:04:46,780
But it's kind of halfway between national and international.

1361
01:04:46,780 --> 01:04:48,780
I'll start with the U.S.

1362
01:04:48,780 --> 01:04:50,780
I mentioned that in the 1930s,

1363
01:04:50,780 --> 01:04:52,780
there was a big step up

1364
01:04:52,780 --> 01:04:54,780
in regulation with the New Deal,

1365
01:04:54,780 --> 01:04:57,780
creating the Securities and Exchange Commission.

